175   C  TITL$Nuclear Structure and Decay Data for A=175 Isobars                
175   c  AUTH$Jin Wu                                                            
175   c  INST$National Nuclear Data Center,                                     
175  #c       Brookhaven National Laboratory,                                   
175  #c       Upton, New York 11973, USA.                                       
175   C  CIT$NDS 206, 1 (2025)                                                  
175   c  ABST$Nuclear structure data for 15 known nuclides of mass number 175   
175  2c  (Dy, Ho, Er, Tm, Yb, Lu, Hf, Ta, W, Re, Os, Ir, Pt, Au, Hg) have been  
175  3c  evaluated, which supersedes the earlier work done by M. Shamsuzzoha    
175  4c  Basunia (2004Ba89), published in Nuclear Data Sheets 102, 719 (2004).  
175   c  CUT$Literature available up to October 29, 2024 has been consulted,    
175  2c  with the help of the primary bibliographic source, the NSR database    
175  3c  (2011Pr03) available at Brookhaven National Laboratory web page:       
175  #c  www.nndc.bnl.gov/nsr/                                                  
175   c  COMM$The evaluation is carried out through a suite of current versions 
175  2c  of computer codes available at the IAEA-Nuclear Data Sheets web page   
175  3c  of ENSDF codes (https://www-nds.iaea.org/public/ensdf_pgm):            
175  4c  FormatCheck, Java_NDS, KenumberCheck, AME-viewer, GLSC, BetaShape,     
175  6c  AlphaHF, RadiationReport, Java-RULER, BrIcc, BrIccMixing, and          
175  7c  ConsistencyCheck.The alpha hindrance factors (HF) presented in this    
175  8c  evaluation have been calculated using values of radius parameter       
175  9c  (r{-0}) interpolated from those even-even adjacent nuclei given in     
175 10c  2020Si16.                                                              
175   c  ACKN$The previous evaluations (2004Ba89, 1993Ma79, 1976Mi07) and       
175  2c  compilations in the XUNDL database provided valuable resources for the 
175  3c  present work.  The evaluator is highly grateful to E.A. McCutchan and  
175  4c  J. Chen for their assistance and patient guidance throughout this      
175  5c  evaluation and also wish to thank the reviewer for comments and        
175   c  PERM$Contact: jwu2@bnl.gov FUND$ Work supported by the Office of       
175  2c  Nuclear Physics, Office of Science, U.S. Department of Energy under    
175  xc  Contract No. |DE-AC02-98CH10946                                        

175DY    ADOPTED LEVELS                                          25NDS    202511
175DY  H TYP=FUL$AUT=Jin Wu$CIT=NDS 206, 1 (2025)$CUT=29-Oct-2024$              
175DY  Q 6570      SY 3670   SY 12050  SY -2950   SY   2021WA16                 
175DY cQ $|DQ(|b{+-})=640, |DS(n)=710, |DS(p)=710, |DQ(|a)=710 (2021Wa16)       
175DY cQ $S(2n)=9130 {I640 (syst)}, Q(|b{+-}n)=1070 {I580 (syst)} (2021Wa16)    
175DY c  2018Fu08, 2018Sh11: {+175}Dy produced and identified at the RIBF-RIKEN 
175DY2c  facility using the {+9}Be({+238}U,F) reaction with a {+238}U{+86+}     
175DY3c  beam of E=345 MeV/nucleon produced by the RIBF accelerator complex,    
175DY4c  using a target of {+9}Be with a thickness of 2.92 mm. Nuclidic         
175DY5c  identification (PID) was made by determining the atomic number Z and   
175DY6c  mass-to-charge (A/Q) ratio of the ions using magnetic rigidity,        
175DY7c  time-of-flight, and energy loss (tof-B|r-|DE method) using the BigRIPS 
175DY8c  fragment separator. The time-of-flight was measured using thin plastic 
175DY9c  scintillators placed at focal planes of the BigRIPS. The B|r values    
175DYac  were deduced from trajectory reconstruction of measured position and   
175DYbc  angle of fragments at each focal plane using parallel plate avalanche  
175DYcc  counters (PPACs). The |DE measurement was conducted using a Si stack   
175DYdc  with 14 layers of Si PIN detectors, surrounded by four HPGe clovers    
175DYec  for isomer tagging by detecting delayed gamma rays from known isomeric 
175DYfc  states in fission fragments. Comparison of measured cross sections     
175DYgc  with theoretical calculations using LISE++ abrasion-fission (AF)       
175DYxc  model.                                                                 
175DY  L 0                                                                      
175DY2 L %B-=100$%B-N=?                                                         
175DY cL $Only |b{+-} decay mode is expected, followed by delayed-neutron       
175DY2cL decay, thus 100% |b{+-} decay is assigned by inference.                
175DY cL $Theoretical T{-1/2}=1.026 s, %|b{+-}n=0 (2019Mo01)                    
175DY cL $Theoretical T{-1/2}=2.16 s, %|b{+-}n=0.3 (2016Ma12)                   
175DY cL $A total of six counts were assigned to {+175}Dy for spectrometer      
175DY2cL setting on Pr, and 22 counts for setting on Gd.                        
175DY cL $Production |s=57 pb {I12} for Gd setting, with 50% systematic         
175DY2cL uncertainty on |s values (2018Fu08)                                    
175DY cL E$it is assumed that the observed events correspond to ground-state    
175DY2cL activity.                                                              
175DY cL J$1/2- from |W{-|n}=1/2- orbital proposed in 2019Mo01                  
175DY cL T$lower limit from time-of-flight of 550 ns given in 2018Fu08. Actual  
175DY2cL half-life is expected to be much longer as suggested by the            
175DY3cL theoretical values of 1.026 s (2019Mo01) and 2.16 s (2016Ma12). From a 
175DY4cL general decreasing trend of half-lives with increasing neutron number, 
175DY5cL T{-1/2} for {+175}Dy is expected to be <2 s, based on measured         
175DY6cL half-lives of 4.1 s for {+171}Dy, 3.9 s for {+172}Dy, and 1.43 s for   
175DY7cL {+173}Dy, available in literature.                                     

175HO    ADOPTED LEVELS                                          25NDS    202511
175HO  H TYP=FUL$AUT=Jin Wu$CIT=NDS 206, 1 (2025)$CUT=29-Oct-2024$              
175HO  Q 5350      SY 5500   SY 9460   SY -1960   SY   2021WA16                 
175HO cQ $|DQ(|b{+-})=570, |DS(n)=500, |DS(p)=640, |DQ(|a)=570 (2021Wa16)       
175HO cQ $S(2n)=10090 {I500 (syst)}, S(2p)=21370 {I640 (syst)}, Q(|b{+-}n)=580  
175HO2cQ {I500 (syst)} (2021Wa16)                                               
175HO c  2012Ku26: {+175}Ho produced and identified in {+9}Be({+238}U,F), E=1   
175HO2c  GeV/nucleon reaction using SIS-18 synchrotron facility at GSI.         
175HO3c  Target=1.6 g/cm{+2} {+9}Be placed at the entrance of projectile        
175HO4c  Fragment Separator (FRS). Particle identification was achieved by      
175HO5c  event-by-event in-flight analysis of time-of-flight, energy loss       
175HO6c  measurement, and magnetic rigidity (tof-|DE-B|r). Time-of-flight       
175HO7c  measured using two plastic scintillation detectors, energy loss or     
175HO8c  deposit by ionization chambers (MUSIC), and magnetic rigidity by four  
175HO9c  time-projection chambers (TPC). Isomer tagging method for known |ms    
175HOac  isomers was used to verify event-by-event identification and           
175HObc  in-flight separation of new isotopes. Gamma rays from the known        
175HOcc  isomers were recorded in coincidence with the incoming ions using      
175HOdc  either the RISING array of Ge detectors at GSI or only two Ge          
175HOec  detectors, a stopper foil and a scintillator for veto signal.          
175HOfc  Measured production cross section. Comparison of measured |s with      
175HOgc  predictions from ABRABLA model and EPAX-3 model                        
175HO c  2017Wu04: The {+175}Ho nuclide was produced at the RIBF-RIKEN          
175HO2c  facility using the {+9}Be({+238}U,F) reaction at E=345 MeV/nucleon.    
175HO3c  Two experiments, optimized for the transmission of {+158}Nd and        
175HO4c  {+170}Dy ions, were carried out with average beam intensities of 7     
175HO5c  pnA and 12 pnA, respectively. The separation and identification of     
175HO6c  the nuclide of interest was made in the BigRIPS separator by           
175HO7c  determining the atomic number and the mass-to-charge ratio of the ion  
175HO8c  using the tof-B|r-|DE method. The reaction products were transported   
175HO9c  through the ZeroDegree Spectrometer and implanted into the             
175HOac  beta-counting system WAS3ABi surrounded by the EURICA array            
175HObc  comprising of 84 HPGe detectors. The typical implantation rate was     
175HOcc  about 100 ions/s. Measured: implanted ion-|b{+-}(t), implanted         
175HOdc  ion-|b{+-}-|g(t) and implanted ions-|g(t) correlations. Deduced:       
175HOec  T{-1/2}.                                                               
175HO  L 0.0          (7/2-)            1.9 S     6                             
175HO2 L %B-=100$%B-N=?                                                         
175HO cL %B-$Only |b{+-} decay mode is expected.                                
175HO cL J$From systematics of known quasiparticle states in neighboring        
175HO2cL nuclei and the proposed configuration (by F. G. Kondev). The           
175HO3cL assignment is tentative.                                               
175HO cL T$From 2017Wu04, using a fit to the implanted ion-|b{+-}(t) spectrum   
175HO2cL using the least-squares and maximum-likelihood methods. The data       
175HO3cL analysis included contributions from the parent, daughter and          
175HO4cL ground-daughter decays, as well as a constant background. A value of   
175HO5cL 1.88 s {I55} is quoted in 2017Wu04                                     
175HO cL CONF$|p7/2[523] Nilsson orbital, based on systematics of known         
175HO2cL structures in neighboring, well-deformed nuclei (by F. G. Kondev).     
175HO3cL The assignment is tentative.                                           
175HO cL $Production |s(at 1 GeV/nucleon)=22 nb {I2} (2012Ku26)                 

